Dear Congressman Pete Aguilar,

My name is Alexander Miller, and I live in a licensed room-and-board home in San Bernardino.

Because meals are provided by the home, I am not eligible for SNAP food benefits. At the same time, I have diabetes, and many of the meals that are routinely served are not appropriate for managing my condition.

My physician recommends healthier alternatives, including plain oatmeal and low-sugar cereals instead of sugary cereals; brown rice, wild rice, or quinoa instead of white rice; lean grilled chicken breasts, fish fillets, turkey, and lean cuts of beef; vegetable soups and bean soups instead of instant noodles; fresh vegetables and salads; plain popcorn, nuts, and low-sugar yogurt for snacks; and beverages such as water, unsweetened tea, kefir, low-sugar kombucha, and Coke Zero or other zero-sugar beverages.

The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services has promoted themes such as “Make America Healthy Again,” “Eat Real Food,” “Get Active,” “Live Real Life,” and “Take Back Your Health.” I support the goal of helping Americans make healthier choices, but my living situation makes it difficult to follow that advice.

Because I am not eligible for SNAP, I have very limited ability to obtain the healthier foods my doctor recommends. I depend almost entirely on the meals provided where I live.

I respectfully ask that you consider ways to help people with medically necessary dietary needs who live in room-and-board homes and therefore do not qualify for SNAP benefits. Even modest changes could make a meaningful difference in helping people with diabetes and other chronic conditions remain healthy.

Thank you for your time, your service to our community, and your consideration of this issue.

Sincerely,

Alexander Miller

